Arduino-Powered Blind Walking Stick

Overview:
I built an Arduino-powered walking stick to help visually impaired people detect obstacles while walking. It uses an ultrasonic sensor to measure the distance to objects and a buzzer to alert the user when an obstacle is nearby. I assembled and tested the physical prototype to see how well it worked.

Components:
-Arduino
-Ultrasonic sensor
-Buzzer
-Jumper wires
-Power bank
-PVC pipe

How It Works:
The ultrasonic sensor detects objects by measuring the distance from the stick.
The Arduino processes the sensor readings.
When an object is within the programmed distance, the buzzer sounds to alert the user.

Programming:
I used Arduino IDE usinb c++ to read distance measurements and control the buzzer.

Testing:
I built and tested the physical prototype to check its ability to detect obstacles and trigger the buzzer. Further testing could help identify its detection limits and improve its reliability.

Future Improvements:
1. Improve obstacle detection accuracy.
2. Make the device smaller and easier to carry.
3. Improve battery life and alert functionality.
4. Add a 3d printed housing to make the final device look more appealing.

Timeline:
I assembled the hardware, programmed the Arduino, and tested the completed prototype.

Resoursce:
